+Computes the 3D world coordinates of voxel centers given their discrete integer indices, bounding box origins, and voxel sizes. It transforms discrete grid indices into continuous spatial coordinates, handling both single-grid and batched scenarios. It differs from sibling methods by explicitly supporting batched inputs with concatenated batch IDs and enforcing specific axis-swapping conventions required by certain rendering pipelines.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(D \times H \times W)$ to generate the full grid. The computation is vectorized and efficient on modern hardware [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(D \times H \times W \times 3)$ to store the center coordinates. For high-resolution grids, this can be significant. Precomputing avoids repeated calculation during rendering [S1, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** The operation is typically performed once during initialization. The cost is negligible compared to the rendering loop [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Simple, deterministic, and fast for regular grids. Provides direct access to world coordinates for any grid index [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Only applicable to regular, axis-aligned grids. Does not support rotated or irregular voxel structures [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** When rendering or training on a fixed, dense voxel grid where world coordinates are needed for each voxel [S1, unofficial].
-- **When not to use:** For sparse voxels, rotated grids, or when memory is constrained and on-the-fly computation is preferred [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(D \cdot H \cdot W)$ to generate the coordinate grid. The meshgrid operation dominates the cost.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(D \cdot H \cdot W \cdot 3)$ to store the resulting tensor. For high-resolution grids, this can be significant.
+- **Strengths:** Provides a direct lookup for voxel centers, avoiding per-ray computation of center positions during rendering.
+- **Limitations:** Memory usage scales cubically with resolution. Not suitable for extremely high-resolution grids without sparse storage strategies.
+- **When to use:** When the voxel grid is static and memory allows storing the full coordinate grid.
+- **When not to use:** When memory is constrained or the grid is too large to fit in RAM.
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting and volume rendering implementations, specifically within the AVM-GS framework [S1, unofficial]. It is a fundamental utility for mapping between discrete grid indices and continuous world space, enabling efficient ray-voxel intersection and attribute lookup [S1, unofficial]. The method is standard in dense grid-based volume representations [S1, unofficial].
+This method is part of the preprocessing pipeline for 3D Gaussian Splatting and related volume rendering techniques. It ensures that voxel centers are accurately mapped to world space for attribute assignment and ray intersection. The implementation is based on the AVM-GS codebase [S1, unofficial].
 
